    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Varanasi was the centre of the Kingdom of Kashi in the time of the Buddha, who first taught at nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, and it became a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> pilgrimage whose ghats on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> are used for bathing and cremation.</li><li>It was raided and ravaged in 1033 and 1194, sacked around 1567, and had temples destroyed on the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a>, and much of the present city was built in the 18th century under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a> patronage.</li><li>A line of rulers took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Benares from 1740, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> acquired the city in 1775, and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> college of 1791 and Banaras <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> University of 1916 continued its record as a centre of learning.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In the 1830s James Prinsep's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> put the number of temples in the city at around 1000, according to the account Rev. M. A. Sherring published in 1868.</p></aside>
    </section>

