    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 800 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Artefacts of this date show the site was inhabited</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">5th century BCE</span><span class="rf-text">The Buddha gives his first teaching at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, near the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 635</span><span class="rf-text">The Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a> visits and describes the city</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1194</span><span class="rf-text">Muizzuddin Muhammad Ghuri ravages the city and many temples are destroyed</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1664</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> builds the Gyanvapi Mosque beside the Kashi Vishwanath Temple</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1740</span><span class="rf-text">Balwant Singh declares himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Benares</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1775</span><span class="rf-text">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> acquires Benares from the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1949</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh">Vibhuti Narayan Singh</a> incorporates the territories of Benares into the United Provinces</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="origins">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Origins</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha.jpg" alt="Gautama Buddha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buddha_in_Sarnath_Museum_(Dhammajak_Mutra).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">พระมหาเทวประภาส วชิรญาณเมธี (ผู้ถ่าย-ปล่อยสัญญาอนุญาตภาพให้นำไปใช้ได้เพื่อการศึกษาโดยอยู่ภา่ยใต้ cc-by-sa-3.0) …, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Varanasi lies on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in the middle of the Ganges valley, between two streams, the Varuna in the north and the Assi in the south, which traditional etymology links to the city's name. Its older name, Kashi, comes from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> root meaning to shine. Excavations in 2014 found artefacts dating back to 800 BCE, and further excavations at Aktha and Ramnagar, two sites near the city, uncovered artefacts dating back to 1800 BCE, which supports the view that the area was inhabited by then. In the time of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gautama-buddha">Gautama Buddha</a> the city was part of the Kingdom of Kashi.</p><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> scriptures Varanasi was founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>. In the story told there, Shiva tore off one of the five heads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a>, and when he came to Varanasi carrying it the head dropped from his hand and disappeared into the ground, which is why the city is considered an extremely holy site. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandavas">Pandavas</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahabharata">Mahabharata</a> are said to have visited it to atone for their sins. It is regarded in Hindu tradition as one of the seven holy cities that can provide moksha, and Hindu texts call it by epithets such as Avimukta, never forsaken by Shiva.</p><p>Other traditions also hold it holy. According to the Buddhavamsa, a Buddhist text, it was the birthplace of the earlier Buddha Kassapa, and Jains believe that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirthankara">Tirthankaras</a> Suparshvanatha and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parshvanatha">Parshvanatha</a> were born there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="growth">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Growth &amp; Great Ages</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/xuanzang.jpg" alt="Xuanzang" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Xuanzang_w.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Varanasi was a centre of religious and artistic activity by the time of the Chinese traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xuanzang">Xuanzang</a>, who visited around 635 CE and recorded that the city extended for about 5 km along the western bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>. He wrote that it had some 30 temples with about 30 monks. Its religious importance grew in the 8th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adi-shankara">Adi Shankara</a> established the worship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as an official sect of the city. Through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it remained a centre for intellectuals and theologians, and several major figures of the Bhakti movement were born there, among them Kabir, who was born in 1389, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a>, a 15th-century reformer, mystic and poet.</p><p>Under Mughal rule <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputs">Rajput</a> nobles, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-i">Raja Man Singh</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kachhwaha">Kachwaha</a> rulers of Amber, rebuilt temples and ghats, and the Raja of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaipur">Jaipur</a> established the Annapurna Mandir. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guru-nanak">Guru Nanak</a> visited for Maha Shivaratri in 1507. Much of the modern city was built in the 18th century, financed especially by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhumihar">Bhumihar</a> rulers, and many of the ghats were built under Maratha patronage. In 1737 a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jantar-mantar">Jantar Mantar</a> observatory was built above the ghats. Rev. M. A. Sherring wrote in 1868 that a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> by James Prinsep put the number of temples at around 1000 in the 1830s. The Varanasi Municipal Board was established in 1867.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a> weaving, brocades with gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> thread, carpets and brassware were among the city's crafts, and weaving was typically done in the household.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rulers">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Who Ruled It</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh.jpg" alt="Vibhuti Narayan Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh">Vibhuti Narayan Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaja_Vibhuti_Narayan_Singh_of_Banaras.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In the first millennium BCE Kashi was a kingdom of its own. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> the city came under the rule of other powers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chandradeva">Chandradeva</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gahadavala-dynasty">Gahadavala dynasty</a>, made Banaras a second capital in 1090. After the battle near Jamuna in 1194 the Ghurid conqueror Muizzuddin Muhammad Ghuri ravaged it. It later came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a>.</p><p>The Kingdom of Benares was given official status by the Mughals in 1737. After the Benares <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamindar">zamindari</a> estate became Banaras State, Balwant Singh of the Narayan dynasty declared himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharaja</a> of Benares in 1740. His clan organisation, with as many as 100,000 men backing the Benares rajas, gave them an advantage against the Nawab of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Oudh</a> in the 1750s and 1760s, and an exhausting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war forced the Nawab to withdraw his main force. The region was ceded to the Benares State, a subordinate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a>, in 1775, and the Company acquired Benares city by the Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a>.</p><p>The city became part of the Benares Division of the Ceded and Conquered Provinces in 1805, the North-Western Provinces in 1836 and the United Provinces in 1902. Benares became a princely state in 1911 with Ramnagar as its capital, though without jurisdiction over the city proper. The kings kept their position until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-independence">Indian independence</a> in 1947, and in 1949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vibhuti-narayan-singh">Vibhuti Narayan Singh</a> incorporated his territories into the United Provinces. Since 1950 the city has been part of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="life">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Life in the City</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sarnath"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sarnath.jpg" alt="Sarnath" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ancient_Buddhist_monasteries_near_Dhamekh_Stupa_Monument_Site,_Sarnath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yusuke Kawasaki, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The ghats, steps leading down the river bank to the water, were where pilgrims performed rituals. Varanasi has at least 84 of them, most used for bathing and for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> worship, and a few used only for cremation. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmibai">Manikarnika</a> and Harishchandra ghats are where Hindus cremate their dead, and the Hindu genealogy registers are kept in the city. Many ghats are associated with Hindu legends. Hindus hold that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahma">Brahma</a> created the Dashashwamedh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghat">Ghat</a>, probably the oldest, to welcome <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a>.</p><p>The city was a place of many faiths. It had an estimated 23,000 temples, and the Kashi Vishwanath Temple, the Sankat Mochan Hanuman Temple and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durga">Durga</a> Temple were the most popular for worship. Muslim weavers settled at Alavipura after the clash of 1034 to 1035, and in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry most weavers were Momin Ansari Muslims. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a>, 10 km to the north-east, is where the Buddha first taught the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dharma">Dharma</a>, and the Buddha is said to have named it as one of four places of pilgrimage for his followers. Guru <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidass</a> Janam Asthan, a religious centre of the Ravidassia religion, marks the birthplace of Ravidas.</p><p>The city was long a centre of learning. The physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sushruta">Sushruta</a>, the primary author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sushruta-samhita">Sushruta Samhita</a>, is said to have lived and practised there in the 5th century BCE. Jonathan Duncan founded the Benares <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> College in 1791, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s oldest Sanskrit college, and Annie Besant founded the Central Hindu College in 1898. The Benares gharana of tabla playing developed in the city, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a> wrote much of the Ramcharitmanas there.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="crises">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Sieges &amp; Disasters</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva.jpg" alt="Gangeyadeva" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva">Gangeyadeva</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kalachuris_of_Tripuri._Gangeya._Circa_1015-1040._Lakshmi_seated_facing_-_%E2%80%9C%C5%9Arimad_Gangeya_Devah%E2%80%9D_in_three_lines.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Classical Numismatic Group, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The first Turkic incursion came in 1033, when Ahmad Nialtagin, a subordinate of Mahmud <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a>, raided the city. His forces plundered the markets and looted gold, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a>, jewels and perfumes, but retreated by midday because of the threat of local resistance. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples were partially destroyed, and the damage was limited as the army stayed briefly. In 1034 to 1035 Malik Afzal Alavi led a section of the army of Syed Salar Masud to Varanasi and was defeated near the site of the Masjid-e-Ganj-e-Shahidan, possibly by local forces under the Kalachuri ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gangeyadeva">Gangeyadeva</a>.</p><p>In 1194 Muizzuddin Muhammad Ghuri defeated the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayachandra">Jayachandra</a> near Jamuna and then ravaged the city, and many temples were destroyed. Around 1567 the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akbar">Akbar</a> sacked Varanasi on his march from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>. In 1656 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aurangzeb">Aurangzeb</a> ordered the destruction of many temples and the building of mosques, and the city experienced a temporary setback.</p><p>Two mosques stand where Hindu temples are said to have been. Accounts of the Gyanvapi Mosque hold that Aurangzeb built it in 1664 after destroying a Hindu temple, and that remains of that temple can be seen in its foundation, its columns and its rear. Accounts of the Alamgir Mosque hold that Aurangzeb built it in 1669 on the ruins of the Bindu Madhav temple. Many of the city's mosques are said to have been built from the components of destroyed Hindu shrines. The Kashi Vishwanath Temple was destroyed and rebuilt several times.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh.jpg" alt="Ranjit Singh" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maharaj_Ranjit_Singh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Colonel James Skinner (1778-1841), Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kashi Vishwanath Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> stands on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, near the Gyanvapi Mosque, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> accounts name as the temple's original site. The present temple was built in 1780 by Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahilyabai-holkar">Ahilyabai Holkar</a> of Indore, and its two pinnacles were covered in gold donated in 1839 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ranjit-singh">Ranjit Singh</a>, the ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. Ramnagar Fort, built in the 18th century by Balwant Singh, holds the history of the kings of Benares and a museum. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramlila">Ramlila</a> of Ramnagar, sponsored by the Kashi Naresh, was begun around 1830 by Udit Narayan Singh.</p><p>The city's writers and musicians include Kabir, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravidas">Ravidas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tulsidas">Tulsidas</a>, Premchand and the shehnai player Bismillah Khan. Its educational record continued with Banaras Hindu University, founded in 1916 by Annie Besant and Madan Mohan Malaviya as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first modern residential university, and Kashi Vidyapith, established in 1921 in response to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a>'s non-cooperation movement. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-twain">Mark Twain</a> wrote in 1897 that Benares was older than history, older than tradition and older even than legend.</p><p>Varanasi is now a city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. The urban agglomeration had a population of 1,435,113 at the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and nearly 70 per cent of the people follow Hinduism, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> the largest minority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a>, Jains, Buddhists and Christians also live there, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> remains a place of Buddhist pilgrimage.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Varanasi, also called Kashi or Benares, is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a>, Buddhists and Jains hold holy. Inhabited by about 800 BCE, it was ravaged by successive conquerors and ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja">Maharajas</a> of Benares, and it remained a centre of learning.</em></p>

